<sect> inout (Revision 1.6 -- 16/07/1998)
<p>
<sect1> File Handling Routines
<p>
<sect2>open_text_file, open_binary_file
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
FILE * open_text_file (const char *fname, const char *mode)
FILE * open_binary_file (const char *fname, const char *mode)
</verb></tscreen>
Open a text file for operations in the <tt/mode/ specified.
On Linux systems, those two functions behave identically.
They have mainly been introduced for porting reasons, so the
main programs using the library functions do not have to be altered.
In addition to the operations performed by
fopen(3), these functions will print an error message, when a file could not
be opened properly.

The parameter <tt/mode/ may be one of the following strings:
<descrip>
<tag/&dquot;r&dquot;/ for reading,
<tag/&dquot;w&dquot;/ for writing,
<tag/&dquot;w+&dquot;/ for appending,
<tag/&dquot;r+&dquot;/ for reading and writing,
</descrip>
or any other mode accepted by fopen(3).

The functions return a pointer to the stream of the file <tt/fname/
in the case it could be opened in the desired <tt/mode/.
If an error occurred while
opening the file, these functions will return a <bf/NULL/ pointer.

<sect2>erase_file
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int erase_file (const char *filename)
</verb></tscreen>
This function erases the file <tt/filename/ when it exists. It is
an extension to unlink(2) by additionally printing a proper error message, 
when a file could not be removed or be found. It will return the 
error code, that it received by calling unlink(2).

<sect2>iscomment
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int iscomment (const char *line)
</verb></tscreen>
This function returns 1, if the line <tt/*line/ is a comment,
i.e. if the line starts with a '#' or is all blank. If not, the return
value is 0.

<sect2>openzipped
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
FILE *openzipped (const char *fname, int *ispipe)
</verb></tscreen>
This function checks, whether the file *fp is a zipped file by looking at its magic *fp, it must already be open for reading!
If the file is zipped, the function closes it, and a pipe on the
unzipped file is passed back. If the file is unzipped, the filepointer
is left untouched.
Return values: TRUE if zipped, FALSE if unzipped, EOF on error

<sect1> Read Operations
<p>
<sect2>read_comment
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int read_comment (FILE *fpin, const int idchar)
</verb></tscreen>
Overread all characters up to a comment specifier.
<p>This function reads bytes from a stream specified by <tt/fpin/
until it finds a character identical to <tt/idchar/
or until it reaches end of file.
The filepointer of <tt/fpin/ will point to the subsequent character of
<tt/idchar/ upon return from the function.

<bf/read_comment/ returns an error code of zero, when the character was found.
<bf/EOF/ is returned as an error code when the end of file
is reached or any other file reading error occurred.

<sect2>overread_comment
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int overread_comment (FILE *fpin, FILE *fpout, const int commentchar)
</verb></tscreen>
Overread comment lines, assuming next char read is first of a line.
<p>This function reads entire lines from a stream specified by <tt/fpin/
until it finds a not beginning with a character identical to <tt/commentchar/
or until it reaches end of file.
The filepointer of <tt/fpin/ will point to the first character of
a line not beginning with <tt/commentchar/ upon return from the function.

If <tt/fpout/ is not a <bf/NULL/ pointer, the skipped comment lines will be
output to that stream, otherwise they are silently discarded.

<bf/overread_comment/ returns an error code of zero upon success.
<bf/EOF/ is returned as an error code when the end of file
is reached or any other file reading error occurred.

<sect2>read_option
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
char * read_option (FILE *fpin, const int idchar)
</verb></tscreen>
Read option entry following a specific character.
<p>This function reads bytes from a stream specified by <tt/fpin/
until it finds a character identical to <tt/idchar/ 
or until it reaches end of file.

With <bf/read_option/ the remainder of the current line, starting with
the character following the <tt/idchar/  up to and including the newline 
character is stored into a newly allocated string.

<bf/read_option/ returns a pointer to this newly allocated string containing
the option information, unless an error occurred while
reading from the file. In case of an error or upon reaching
the end of file, it returns a <bf/NULL/ pointer.

<sect2>read_soption
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
char * read_soption (FILE *fpin, const int idchar)
</verb></tscreen>
Read string option entry following a specific character.
<p>This function reads bytes from a stream specified by <tt/fpin/
until it finds a character identical to <tt/idchar/ 
or until it reaches end of file.

With <bf/read_soption/, only one string is returned as with the `&percnt;s' template
when using sscanf(3).

All preceeding white space will be overread, and
the string will be read up to a following white space
character. The returned string will not contain any of the
white space characters. Any subsequent strings or characters
following until the end of line will be dropped. 

<bf/read_soption/ returns a pointer to this newly allocated string containing
the option information, unless an error occurred while
reading from the file. In case of an error or upon reaching
the end of file, it returns a <bf/NULL/ pointer. It will 
also return a null pointer, when no string could be found following the
<tt/idchar/.

The function <bf/read_soption/ was primarily introduced to quickly read 
filenames as option contents from an input file. The return
value may thus easily be checked for the existence of such
an item.

<sect2>read_line_static
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int read_line_static (FILE *fpin, char *s, const int size)
</verb></tscreen>
       This function  reads  in at most one less than <tt/size/ 
       characters from
       <tt/fpin/ and stores them into the buffer pointed  to  by  <tt/s/.
       Reading  stops after an EOF or a newline.  If a newline is
       read, it is stored into the  buffer.   A  '&bsol;0'  is  stored
       after the last character in the buffer.

The buffer must be previously allocated to hold at least <tt/size/
characters.

The return value is the length of the string read from the file, or
<bf/EOF/ on error or when an end of file occurs while no characters
have been read.

<sect2>read_line
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int read_line (FILE *fpin, char **lineptr)
</verb></tscreen>
<bf/The use of this function is deprecated!/ It will be removed in
the future. Use <it/read_line_static/ instead.
<p>This function reads bytes from a stream specified by <tt/fpin/
until it finds a newline character or until it reaches end of file.
The pointer to the newly allocated string of read characters including 
the newline character
is stored into the location passed by <tt/lineptr/.

The function returns the size of newly allocated string or an <bf/EOF/,
when the end of file was reached or an error occurred.

<sect2>read_delim<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int read_delim (FILE *fpin, int delimiter, char **lineptr)
</verb></tscreen>
This function reads the current line of <tt/*fpin/ up to a
<tt/delimiter/. The result is returned in <tt/*lineptr/.

<sect1>String Operations (e.g. for filenames)
<p>
<sect2>remove_extension
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void remove_extension (char *filename)
</verb></tscreen>
This function will truncate the string <tt/filename/
by replacing the last dot in the string by a zero character.
The string passed to this function will be modified!

<sect2>get_extension
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
char * get_extension (const char *filename)
</verb></tscreen>
This function returns a pointer to a newly allocated string containing the
extension including the leading dot. The string <tt/filename/
that it is passed as an argument will not be altered!
The return value is a pointer to a newly allocated string containing the
extension including the leading dot.
When no extension was present in <tt/filename/, this function will return a
<bf/NULL/ pointer.

<sect2>add_extension
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
char * add_extension (const char *oldname, const char *ext)
</verb></tscreen>
This function returns a pointer to a newly allocated string containing the
filename with a new extension.

<sect2>get_fname_wopath
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
char * get_fname_wopath (const char *filename)
</verb></tscreen>
This function returns a new allocated string
containing the filename excluding the leading path.
If <tt/filename/ does not contain a leading path, the function
returns a newly allocated string with a copy of <tt/filename/.

<sect2>strpos_withblanks
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int strpos_withblanks(const char *s, const int count)
</verb></tscreen>
This function parses the string <tt/s/ from the position <tt/count/ backwards
until it finds a non-space character. It returns the position of the
first whitespace character as defined by isspace(3).

For use with returned int of &percnt;n on sscanf(3).

<sect2>uppercase
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void uppercase (char *s)
</verb></tscreen>
This function replaces every lowercase character in <tt/s/ into
its uppercase counterpart. This function will fail, if the string is
not null terminated. The string <tt/s/ will be modified.

<sect2>lowercase
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void lowercase (char *s)
</verb></tscreen>
This function replaces every uppercase character in <tt/s/ into
its lowercase counterpart. This function will fail, if the string is
not null terminated. The string <tt/s/ will be modified.

<sect2>replacechar
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void replacechar (char *s, int oldc, int newc)
</verb></tscreen>
This function replaces every <tt/oldc/ character in <tt/s/ into
a <tt/newc/ character. This function will fail, if the string is
not null terminated. The string <tt/s/ will be modified.

<sect2>extract_values/extract_values_static
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int extract_values (const char *line, char **prefix,
		    const unsigned int prefix_size, 
		    stuct coordcol *infix, char **suffix,
		    const unsigned long linecount)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
This funcion extracts columns from an input line <tt/*line/ and stores
them in <tt/*infix/. <tt/Prefix_size/ characters, counted from the
beginning of the line, are stored in <tt/**prefix/. The variable
<tt/linecount/ is not changed or increased in this function. If the extraction was successfull, the return value is 0.
<tscreen><verb>
int extract_values_static (const char *line, char [prefix],
		    const int prefix_size, 
		    stuct scoordcol *infix, char [suffix],
		    const int max_suffix_size, const unsigned long linecount)
</verb></tscreen>
This function extracts columns from an input line <tt/*line/ and stores them in
<tt/*infix/. <tt/Prefix_size/ characters, counted from the beginning
of the line, are stored in <tt/prefix/, <tt/max_suffix_size/
characters, counted from the end of the line, in <tt/suffix/. The variable
<tt/linecount/ is not changed or increased in this function. If the
extraction was successful, the return value is 0. The static version
of this function is for faster processing without new memory allocation.










